;; The ClojureScript half of the cross-runtime state-root parity check
;; (`torihiki.parity`), runnable from a clean checkout:
;;
;;   KOTOBA_CHECKOUTS=<dir-of-sibling-checkouts> script/nbb-parity.sh
;;
;; `script/nbb-leg.sh` (which that wrapper calls) builds the classpath from
;; the deps.edn pins and runs this file through `kbb --backend sci`; the JVM
;; half is `script/jvm-parity.sh`. The two must print the same digests.
;;
;; ## Why this file exists
;;
;; The nbb half of the parity check lived in prose — `torihiki.parity`'s
;; docstring and the README's Platform section named it as a one-liner with a
;; hand-rolled classpath:
;;
;;   kbb --backend sci --classpath "src:<path-to>/bytes/src" -e "(require '[torihiki.parity :as p]) (p/report)"
;;
;; and the same prose named the JVM half as `kbb -M:parity`. All three
;; invocations were measured failing on 2026-10-24, the day this file landed
;; (see evidence/2026-10-24-parity-leg-lands-in-repo.md):
;;
;;   kbb -M:parity            -> "Unable to resolve symbol: torihiki.parity/-main"
;;                               (the alias's :main-opts name `-main`, which is
;;                               `#?(:clj ...)` only — the SCI engine has none)
;;   clojure -M:parity        -> "Could not locate torihiki/parity__init.class"
;;                               (the `.cljk` sources are invisible to the
;;                               JVM; script/jvm-leg.sh is the recipe)
;;   the -e one-liner, naive  -> "Could not find namespace: merkle-sum.core"
;;   classpath fill              (the pins ARE the classpath; prose
;;                               placeholders are not)
;;
;; So the check existed, pinned in `torihiki.commit-test`, and the commands to
;; run it did not work — the same rot that took the two-runtime suite claim
;; down twice before script/jvm-test.sh and script/nbb-test.sh landed. The
;; suite pins what the digests ARE (a deliberate encoding change moves them,
;; with a comment, in commit_test); this file exists so both runtimes can be
;; asked to PRINT them, which is the cross-runtime claim.
;;
;; The output must be byte-identical to the JVM half's: same function, same
;; printlns. If the two outputs drift, the runtimes disagree on the state
;; root and a browser cannot verify a block a JVM validator produced.
(ns torihiki-parity-on-nbb
  (:require [torihiki.parity :as p]))

(p/report)
